ralph/codex-output/iteration-000029-20260518-224145.log:768:- Security-verification SSRF blocklist pattern, validate-port instance (BE-0349 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{ssrf.go, ssrf_static_test.go, ssrf_test.go, fuzz_test.go, AGENTS.md}; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST in the validate package — confirms the template scales to "this pure validator MUST funnel its parsed host through a named blocklist helper" invariants in addition to single-function-hardening (BE-0346), per-call-site predicate scans (BE-0344/0345/0347), and absence-of-API scans (BE-0348). Six SSRF-flavoured rules this instance surfaces that the earlier five do NOT capture: (a) The analyzer is a per-function call-presence scan rather than a per-call-site scan or absence-of-API scan: each of {URL, GitRepoURL} is parsed via findFuncDecl, its body walked with ast.Inspect, and the predicate "calls disallowedSSRFHost by exact identifier" must hold. Exact-identifier matching is the load-bearing rule — a synthetic "calls disallowedHost (look-alike)" fixture is in the self-check so a future refactor that renames the helper inside one validator (but not the other) fails the static guard; substring or prefix matching here would mask the regression. (b) The reason text is a closed set, NOT a templated string with the host interpolated. Nine canonical reasons (8 IP-range classifications + 1 hostname classification) live in package-private constants emitted by ipReason / disallowedSSRFHost. The runtime test pins the set via canonicalSSRFReasons + isCanonicalSSRFReason and asserts every rejection is set-membership equal — strictly stronger than substring redaction checks, because words like "localhost" / "metadata" / "loopback" / "private" appear in the classification text BY DESIGN and would false-positive a substring "host did not appear in reason" guard. The BE-0343 redaction-fixture-collision pattern (choose canary values that don't collide with stable reason phrases) is the predecessor; the closed-set assertion is the next-strongest form. (c) Reject IP literals via net.ParseIP first, then hostnames; the order matters because an IP literal can never be made safe by downstream DNS, so the unconditional-reject decision happens before any hostname rule applies. The IP classifier uses Go stdlib's IsUnspecified / IsLoopback / IsLinkLocalUnicast / IsLinkLocalMulticast / IsMulticast / IsPrivate as the first cut, then adds the four IPv4 ranges stdlib does NOT classify (CGNAT 100.64/10, benchmark 198.18/15, limited broadcast 255.255.255.255 — 169.254/16 is already covered by IsLinkLocalUnicast). Whitelisting IsPrivate alone would miss the cloud-metadata range (169.254.169.254 is link-local, not private). (d) Hostname forbidden set is asymmetric: an exact-match map (forbiddenSSRFHostnames) for {localhost, ip6-localhost, ip6-loopback, metadata, metadata.google.internal, metadata.aws.internal, 169.254.169.254} and a leading-dot suffix list (forbiddenSSRFHostSuffixes) for {.localhost, .internal, .local, .localdomain}. The leading dot on suffixes is required — a bare ".local" suffix without the dot would false-positive on "flocal.example.com". TestSSRFHostnameListsAreCanonical is a sealing-trap pinning required entries AND the leading-dot invariant; dropping any documented entry fails the build. (e) GitRepoURL has THREE entry shapes (https:// / ssh:// / scp-like git@host:path) and the SSRF check must fire on ALL THREE: the analyzer pins the presence of ONE disallowedSSRFHost call in the function body, but the runtime test exercises each shape independently (https loopback, ssh link-local v6, scp .internal, etc.) so a future split that drops the call from the scp branch alone — analyzer still happy because the https branch still has it — surfaces in the runtime table. The lesson: presence-of-call analyzers are necessary but not sufficient; per-shape runtime coverage is the second wall. (f) IPv6 literals in URL form MUST use the bracketed `[fd00::1]` shape because url.Parse with a colon-bearing v6 address outside brackets parses the colons as port separators; the runtime test fixtures call this out explicitly. The disallowedSSRFHost helper strips one set of surrounding brackets so it accepts BOTH the URL-extracted (already unbracketed) form AND a literal bracketed form a caller might supply directly. Layered defence is explicit in AGENTS.md: this is the PURE validator layer (no DNS) and a second wall in the HTTP/git client at fetch time is REQUIRED to catch DNS-rebinding (a public name resolving to an internal IP at fetch time). The validator is the first wall; the runtime resolved-IP re-check is a separate story.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000029-20260518-224145.log:781:- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.
