ralph/codex-output/iteration-000098-20260519-065806.log:1567:- Log-redaction fuzz harness, single-test runtime-only variant (BE-0359 pattern, internal/output/redact_fuzz_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Log Redaction Fuzzing" section + internal/output/AGENTS.md "Log redaction fuzzing (BE-0359)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the single-test runtime-only shape called out in the BE-0344 pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog, e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts). Six load-bearing rules: (1) FUZZ-MARKER design — when proving a structural redaction rule scrubs the value-half of a transport pattern, bracket the fuzz-supplied value with a unique fixed marker (`"FUZZAUTHMARKERXYZ"`, `"FUZZQRYMARKERZYX"`) and assert the marker never appears in the redacted output. The marker is the leak detector, independent of the value's bytes. Asserting that the value-bytes do not appear false-positives whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the test's static suffix (e.g. `"example"` if the test constructs `Host: example.com`). The marker pattern is reusable for ANY future fuzz target that proves "structural rule X scrubs the value half of transport Y" — cookies, refresh tokens, future header/query patterns. (2) REGEX-VALUE-CLASS gotcha — Go's `regexp` `\s` is exactly `[\t\n\f\r ]`; vertical-tab (`\v`) is NOT in `\s` but form-feed (`\f`) IS. When constructing a fuzz value that must traverse a regex value class without being cut short, strip every character in the regex's exclusion set including every member of Go's `\s`. The first revision missed `\f` and a seed containing it ended the regex mid-value, surviving the marker bracket and producing a false-positive leak. (3) DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH interactions — when two structural rules can both fire on a fuzz value (e.g. a query value containing `AUTHORIZATION:` triggers the bearer regex which eats the rest of the URL), assert ONLY the safety property (marker absent), NOT cosmetic properties (non-secret params survive). Document the interaction in the test file so a future reader understands why the survival assertion is absent. (4) SENTINEL-OVERLAP scope-out — `Redactor.Redact` uses `strings.ReplaceAll(s, secret, Sentinel)`; if `secret` is a substring of `Sentinel` (e.g. `"REDA"` inside `"[REDACTED]"`), the second pass rewrites the freshly-inserted sentinel substring and the function is NOT idempotent. Skip these inputs in fuzz AND pin the exclusion in a regression test (`TestRedactor_FuzzScopeExclusionsAreReal`) so a future redactor fix (single-pass regex substitution that does not re-scan substituted text) deliberately breaks that test and forces a coordinated update to the fuzz scope, the SECURITY.md section, and the AGENTS.md note. (5) SEED-CORPUS vs `-fuzz` — `go test ./...` runs every seed in `f.Add` once, so the seed corpus is the regression net every commit exercises; `-fuzz` is operator-opt-in. When adding a new redaction rule, the worked pattern is: append a seed input to `redactFuzzSeeds` that triggers the new rule, add a fuzz target if the rule has a distinct structural shape, and update both the AGENTS.md and SECURITY.md sections at the same time. (6) `-fuzztime` SHUTDOWN quirk — `go test -fuzz=… -fuzztime=5s` with the default 16 workers sometimes reports `context deadline exceeded` at shutdown; this is a harness-internal timing quirk, not a real fuzz failure (no `testdata/fuzz/…` corpus file is written). Re-running with `-parallel=2` makes the shutdown cleaner. Forward-applicable to any future security-verification story whose invariant has no AST shape to gate (BE-0360 environment-variable redaction if the rule is purely "the variables service MUST pass every rendered value through the redactor before logging" with no AST shape; BE-0363 rate-limit bypass resistance; chaos-tests for timeouts; idempotency replay).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000098-20260519-065806.log:1571:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000098-20260519-065806.log:1573:- Inbound-hostname domain-takeover guard, symmetric to the SSRF outbound-URL guard (BE-0350 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{domain_takeover.go, domain_takeover_static_test.go, domain_takeover_test.go, AGENTS.md} + network.go Domain() call site + fuzz_test.go FuzzDomain seeds; second worked port of the BE-0349 two-test SSRF template into the validate package, but for a STRUCTURALLY DIFFERENT threat: INBOUND hostnames the platform agrees to ROUTE traffic for, NOT outbound URLs the worker dereferences). Six load-bearing rules the new file added on top of the inherited BE-0349 template: (a) The takeover guard MUST live in a separate helper (`disallowedTakeoverHost`) and a separate static analyzer (`callsDisallowedTakeoverHost`) from the SSRF guard — they share the two-test template but check DISJOINT sets: a host that is legitimate to ATTACH (`myapp.example.com`) may still be illegitimate to FETCH if it resolves to a private IP, and vice versa. Reusing the SSRF helper would collapse both threat models into one, hiding which rejection class applies to which surface. (b) The guard's call site goes AT THE END of `Domain` (after the FQDN/label checks), not at the start. Single-label reserved names (`localhost`, `local`, `internal`, `lan`, etc.) and IPv6 literals (a single label after the `.` split) are rejected by the existing FQDN check BEFORE reaching the guard — so the test table for `TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargets` restricts itself to inputs that actually pass through the helper today (IPv4 dotted literals, multi-label reserved exact-matches like `home.arpa`, suffix-match cases, shared-hosting eTLD bare forms). The single-label reserved names stay in `reservedTakeoverHostnames` as defence-in-depth (a future FQDN-check loosening must still trip the takeover guard) but the runtime regression table only asserts on what's actually reachable, so a missing call site is caught by a FAILING subtest rather than a silently-skipped one. Document the layering explicitly in the file's package-doc block AND in the test file's table-header comment, so the next reader doesn't add an unreachable case and then debug for an hour. (c) The reserved-name lists must DELIBERATELY EXCLUDE the RFC 2606 documentation-only suffixes (`.test`, `.example`, `.invalid`) from the SUFFIX walk while KEEPING them in the EXACT-MATCH map. Reason: those suffixes are not publicly routable so they cannot intercept real customer traffic — they are a squatting threat (bounded by the `service_domains` UNIQUE (hostname, path) constraint) rather than a takeover threat. The httpapi service-domains test fixtures across ~7 files (`api.canonical.example`, `domain-matrix-recognisable-needle.example`, etc.) use `.example` per RFC convention; adding `.example` to the suffix walk would break every one of those tests, and the cost is asymmetric: a real takeover defence would require migrating the fixtures whereas a softer squatting defence is bounded by the UNIQUE constraint and adds no value to either threat model. Document the omission AND the asymmetry in the helper's package-doc, the AGENTS.md section, and the test file's blocked-hosts table comment — so a future reader doesn't "fix" the omission and re-break the fixtures. (d) The wildcard interaction: `Domain` strips the leading `*.` BEFORE my helper sees the host. With wildcards enabled, `*.appspot.com` reduces to `appspot.com` which the exact-match map rejects — a wildcard makes the takeover STRICTLY WORSE (the bare suffix locks one routing entry, the wildcard locks every sub-tenant of that suffix) so the wildcard rejection is load-bearing and gets its own test (`TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargetsForWildcard`). The helper ALSO defensively strips a leading `*.` itself so it remains correct for any future caller that passes the raw value. (e) The redaction backstop (`TestDomainTakeoverReasonsAreValueFree`) must plant the canary as a SUB-LABEL of a reserved-SUFFIX host (`canary + ".lan"`), NOT a reserved-EXACT-MATCH host (`canary + "appspot.com"` would not match the exact-match map). The suffix walk's `strings.HasSuffix(h, suffix)` reduces the entire host to the suffix-class reason without inspecting the canary — so a reason containing the canary proves the validator interpolated the value. The canary string itself MUST be picked to NOT collide with any word in the canonical reason set (no "domain", "namespace", "address", "literal", "hostname") — the BE-0343 redaction lesson is that substring redaction checks false-positive on stable classification phrases. (f) The FuzzDomain seed set added one entry per documented reject category PLUS an accept-branch re-derivation: `net.ParseIP(v) != nil` and `strings.HasSuffix(v, reservedSuffix)` MUST be false for every accepted host. The accept-branch re-derivation is what makes the fuzz target actually catch a regression of the takeover guard — without it, dropping the helper would let IPv4-literal seeds pass through to the accept branch without firing any assertion (the existing FQDN+label re-derivation accepts `8.8.8.8`). The reservedSuffixes slice in the fuzz target is a DUPLICATE of the package-private one — that is the cost of the validate_test (`_test` package) split: a future helper change must update BOTH lists or `TestDomainTakeoverListsAreCanonical` fires for the package list and the fuzz target silently accepts the dropped category. Document the duplication in the seed-loop comment.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000098-20260519-065806.log:1575:- Security-verification SSRF blocklist pattern, validate-port instance (BE-0349 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{ssrf.go, ssrf_static_test.go, ssrf_test.go, fuzz_test.go, AGENTS.md}; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST in the validate package — confirms the template scales to "this pure validator MUST funnel its parsed host through a named blocklist helper" invariants in addition to single-function-hardening (BE-0346), per-call-site predicate scans (BE-0344/0345/0347), and absence-of-API scans (BE-0348). Six SSRF-flavoured rules this instance surfaces that the earlier five do NOT capture: (a) The analyzer is a per-function call-presence scan rather than a per-call-site scan or absence-of-API scan: each of {URL, GitRepoURL} is parsed via findFuncDecl, its body walked with ast.Inspect, and the predicate "calls disallowedSSRFHost by exact identifier" must hold. Exact-identifier matching is the load-bearing rule — a synthetic "calls disallowedHost (look-alike)" fixture is in the self-check so a future refactor that renames the helper inside one validator (but not the other) fails the static guard; substring or prefix matching here would mask the regression. (b) The reason text is a closed set, NOT a templated string with the host interpolated. Nine canonical reasons (8 IP-range classifications + 1 hostname classification) live in package-private constants emitted by ipReason / disallowedSSRFHost. The runtime test pins the set via canonicalSSRFReasons + isCanonicalSSRFReason and asserts every rejection is set-membership equal — strictly stronger than substring redaction checks, because words like "localhost" / "metadata" / "loopback" / "private" appear in the classification text BY DESIGN and would false-positive a substring "host did not appear in reason" guard. The BE-0343 redaction-fixture-collision pattern (choose canary values that don't collide with stable reason phras