ralph/codex-output/iteration-000060-20260519-021413.log:843:- Security-verification SSRF blocklist pattern, validate-port instance (BE-0349 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{ssrf.go, ssrf_static_test.go, ssrf_test.go, fuzz_test.go, AGENTS.md}; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST in the validate package — confirms the template scales to "this pure validator MUST funnel its parsed host through a named blocklist helper" invariants in addition to single-function-hardening (BE-0346), per-call-site predicate scans (BE-0344/0345/0347), and absence-of-API scans (BE-0348). Six SSRF-flavoured rules this instance surfaces that the earlier five do NOT capture: (a) The analyzer is a per-function call-presence scan rather than a per-call-site scan or absence-of-API scan: each of {URL, GitRepoURL} is parsed via findFuncDecl, its body walked with ast.Inspect, and the predicate "calls disallowedSSRFHost by exact identifier" must hold. Exact-identifier matching is the load-bearing rule — a synthetic "calls disallowedHost (look-alike)" fixture is in the self-check so a future refactor that renames the helper inside one validator (but not the other) fails the static guard; substring or prefix matching here would mask the regression. (b) The reason text is a closed set, NOT a templated string with the host interpolated. Nine canonical reasons (8 IP-range classifications + 1 hostname classification) live in package-private constants emitted by ipReason / disallowedSSRFHost. The runtime test pins the set via canonicalSSRFReasons + isCanonicalSSRFReason and asserts every rejection is set-membership equal — strictly stronger than substring redaction checks, because words like "localhost" / "metadata" / "loopback" / "private" appear in the classification text BY DESIGN and would false-positive a substring "host did not appear in reason" guard. The BE-0343 redaction-fixture-collision pattern (choose canary values that don't collide with stable reason phrase