ralph/codex-output/iteration-000131-20260519-120828.log:3859:ralph/progress.txt:210:- Log-redaction fuzz harness, single-test runtime-only variant (BE-0359 pattern, internal/output/redact_fuzz_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Log Redaction Fuzzing" section + internal/output/AGENTS.md "Log redaction fuzzing (BE-0359)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the single-test runtime-only shape called out in the BE-0344 pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose invariant is a runtime contract with NO static analog, e.g. log redaction fuzzing, idempotency replay, chaos tests for timeouts). Six load-bearing rules: (1) FUZZ-MARKER design — when proving a structural redaction rule scrubs the value-half of a transport pattern, bracket the fuzz-supplied value with a unique fixed marker (`"FUZZAUTHMARKERXYZ"`, `"FUZZQRYMARKERZYX"`) and assert the marker never appears in the redacted output. The marker is the leak detector, independent of the value's bytes. Asserting that the value-bytes do not appear false-positives whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the test's static suffix (e.g. `"example"` if the test constructs `Host: example.com`). The marker pattern is reusable for ANY future fuzz target that proves "structural rule X scrubs the value half of transport Y" — cookies, refresh tokens, future header/query patterns. (2) REGEX-VALUE-CLASS gotcha — Go's `regexp` `\s` is exactly `[\t\n\f\r ]`; vertical-tab (`\v`) is NOT in `\s` but form-feed (`\f`) IS. When constructing a fuzz value that must traverse a regex value class without being cut short, strip every character in the regex's exclusion set including every member of Go's `\s`. The first revision missed `\f` and a seed containing it ended the regex mid-value, surviving the marker bracket and producing a false-positive leak. (3) DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH interactions — when two structural rules can both fire on a fuzz value (e.g. a query value containing `AUTHORIZATION:` triggers the bearer regex which eats the rest of the URL), assert ONLY the safety property (marker absent), NOT cosmetic properties (non-secret params survive). Document the interaction in the test file so a future reader understands why the survival assertion is absent. (4) SENTINEL-OVERLAP scope-out — `Redactor.Redact` uses `strings.ReplaceAll(s, secret, Sentinel)`; if `secret` is a substring of `Sentinel` (e.g. `"REDA"` inside `"[REDACTED]"`), the second pass rewrites the freshly-inserted sentinel substring and the function is NOT idempotent. Skip these inputs in fuzz AND pin the exclusion in a regression test (`TestRedactor_FuzzScopeExclusionsAreReal`) so a future redactor fix (single-pass regex substitution that does not re-scan substituted text) deliberately breaks that test and forces a coordinated update to the fuzz scope, the SECURITY.md section, and the AGENTS.md note. (5) SEED-CORPUS vs `-fuzz` — `go test ./...` runs every seed in `f.Add` once, so the seed corpus is the regression net every commit exercises; `-fuzz` is operator-opt-in. When adding a new redaction rule, the worked pattern is: append a seed input to `redactFuzzSeeds` that triggers the new rule, add a fuzz target if the rule has a distinct structural shape, and update both the AGENTS.md and SECURITY.md sections at the same time. (6) `-fuzztime` SHUTDOWN quirk — `go test -fuzz=… -fuzztime=5s` with the default 16 workers sometimes reports `context deadline exceeded` at shutdown; this is a harness-internal timing quirk, not a real fuzz failure (no `testdata/fuzz/…` corpus file is written). Re-running with `-parallel=2` makes the shutdown cleaner. Forward-applicable to any future security-verification story whose invariant has no AST shape to gate (BE-0360 environment-variable redaction if the rule is purely "the variables service MUST pass every rendered value through the redactor before logging" with no AST shape; BE-0363 rate-limit bypass resistance; chaos-tests for timeouts; idempotency replay).
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000131-20260519-120828.log:3861:ralph/progress.txt:214:- Tamper-resistance three-wall verification pattern (BE-0353, internal/controlplane/store/{audit_tamper_static_test.go, audit_test.go::TestAuditUpdateBlockedByDatabaseTriggerWithRestrictViolation} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/audit_immutability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/AGENTS.md "Tamper resistance" subsection; structural template for any future append-only-table story whose immutability is enforced at the database trigger AND the Go repository surface — security events, key-rotation history, billing-ledger entries, fraud alerts, anything where "the record cannot change after write" is a load-bearing contract). The pattern is THREE static-time guards plus ONE runtime probe so a weakening of any one layer is caught by another. Five load-bearing rules: (1) MIGRATION-shape pin (parse the .up.sql via embedded fs and assert four regex shapes: function declared with `ERRCODE = '<sqlstate-name>'`, trigger declared with `BEFORE UPDATE ON <table> FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION <fn>`, the `CREATE TABLE` parenthesised body extracted and scanned for any forbidden column declaration, the `.down.sql` drops both the table AND the function — DROP TABLE alone leaves the function orbiting unused). The column-scan strips `--`-prefixed lines BEFORE matching so an in-body comment like `-- intentionally no updated_at column` does not trip the probe; the matcher anchors on `^(?:\s|,)*<column>\s+[a-zA-Z]` so a bare token mentioned in prose elsewhere does not match. (2) Go-SURFACE pin (parse the implementation .go via the existing mustParseFile helper, walk file.Decls, collect every FuncDecl whose receiver is the named type, and assert the set is exactly the allowed names AND each name does not start with a known mutation-verb prefix `{Update,Delete,Patch,Replace,Modify,Truncate,Remove}` — the verb prefix is a SECOND filter so a future allowed-set drift that re-admits a mutation-shape name still fails); also pin the struct declaration has no mutation-timestamp field `{UpdatedAt,ModifiedAt,RevisedAt,LastModifiedAt}` — the SQL column absence is the migration's job, the Go field absence is the surface's job, both must hold. (3) DML-SCAN pin (walk every non-test `.go` in the owning package, find every `*ast.BasicLit` of `token.STRING` kind, unwrap raw-string and double-quoted shapes, normalise whitespace and case, and reject any literal that contains a forbidden DML substring against the target table; only the carved-out INSERT shape inside the repository's own file is allowed — every other shape is a regression flagged with `file:line: forbidden DML against <table>: "UPDATE <table>" in literal`). The case-insensitive uppercase normalisation handles lowercase shapes that snuck in via copy-paste; the whitespace normalisation handles multi-line literals like `UPDATE\n  audit_events\nSET ...`. Comments are NOT scanned — the rule targets executable code paths and a SQL example in a doc comment is fine. (4) RUNTIME-wire pin (insert a fixture row, attempt every UPDATE shape an operator might try — by id, by tenant id, table-wide without WHERE, partial-column-rewrite, verdict-reclassification — and assert each rejection surfaces a `*pgconn.PgError` with `pgErr.Code == "<sqlstate>"` AND `strings.Contains(pgErr.Message, "<canonical phrase>")` AND the row's columns are byte-identical to the original after the attempted UPDATE; PG's `restrict_violation` is SQLSTATE 23001 NOT 38000 — 38000 is external_routine_exception, an easy typo because both classes start with a 3, verify the actual wire code by running once and reading the failure). Also assert the error message does NOT echo any caller input — the trigger function is value-free by design and a future log path that interpolates the rejected payload would surface here as a redaction regression. (5) SELF-CHECK pin (drive each matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000131-20260519-120828.log:3864:ralph/progress.txt:231:- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.
