-bad inputs in subtests so a future over-tightening that false-positives on real production code AND a future under-tightening that misses a real regression are BOTH caught — the self-check is the only safety net against "I weakened my own analyser without noticing"). Six asymmetries worth knowing: (a) `audit_events` allows direct SQL `DELETE FROM ...` because the design requires ON DELETE CASCADE to work for tenant teardown — so the DML-scan flags DELETE FROM at the Go layer (no app-side delete) but the runtime test does NOT assert DELETE is blocked at the DB layer (it isn't). The migration AGENTS.md section documents this asymmetry so a future reader doesn't try to "fix" it. (b) The wire SQLSTATE for the migration's `RAISE EXCEPTION ... ERRCODE = 'restrict_violation'` is 23001 (class 23 integrity_constraint_violation) — PG remaps the symbolic name to a numeric code at trigger compile time, the SQL pins the name, the runtime test pins the code, both must stay synchronised. (c) Mutation-verb prefix detection deliberately EXCLUDES `Set` and `Get` — too broad to flag without false positives across unrelated repositories — and the closed verb set lives in `isMutationSurfaceMethodName` as a helper rather than inline so adding a new verb is a single change. (d) The struct-field forbidden list does NOT include `UpdatedBy` / `LastEditor` — those are actor-attribution fields, not mutation timestamps; the rule targets writes-to-the-record, not metadata about who touched it. (e) The migration column-scan strips `--` line comments before matching but does NOT strip `/* */` block comments — PG accepts both, but `audit_events.up.sql` uses `--` exclusively and the simpler scanner is enough; if a future migration mixes styles, extend the scanner with the same upper-vs-lower test pattern. (f) The runtime test reuses the existing `auditEventFixture` / `seedOrg` / `appendAudit` helpers from `audit_test.go` so the new test is ~150 lines of mostly cases-and-assertions; the wire-error assertions live in a `*pgconn.PgError` `errors.As` extraction (NOT a string match on err.Error()) so a future error-wrapping change at the pgx layer that preserves the typed error still surfaces the assertion.
ralph/codex-output/iteration-000131-20260519-120828.log:4510:ralph/codex-output/iteration-000018-20260518-180116.log:495:- Operations artefact verification leaf (BE-0409 pattern, internal/controlplane/build/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, api_dockerfile_test.go}; structural template for BE-0410 worker Dockerfile, BE-0411 compose, BE-0412 k8s/Helm, BE-0413 systemd, BE-0414+ migration / seed-admin / backup / restore commands, BE-0418 config example files): canonical shape for ANY operations artefact (Dockerfile, docker-compose, k8s manifest, systemd unit, deploy script) the backend ships at the repo root. One Go test file per artefact in this package (api_dockerfile_test.go, worker_dockerfile_test.go, compose_test.go, ...), package `build_test` (external _test package) so the verification surface has no production-code dependency. Tests locate the artefact via repoRoot(t) which walks up from os.Getwd() until it finds go.mod — hard-coded relative paths break when the test moves. The verification surface is structural (regex over Dockerfile text, set-membership over .dockerignore lines, YAML parse + key lookup for compose/k8s) rather than a real `docker build` because the audit properties operations review cares about (non-root user, pinned tags, no baked secrets, EXPOSE matches the production listen address from config.ProfileProduction's apiAddr default) are textual, not behavioural. Forbidden-secret list MUST track config.Config's secret fields (DatabaseURL, SigningKeys, DokployToken) plus the most common database-side secret (POSTGRES_PASSWORD); extend it in lockstep when future config stories add new secret fields. ENV NAME=VAL, legacy `ENV NAME VAL`, and `ARG NAME=VAL with non-empty default` are ALL three banned forms — a developer who pastes `ARG YALLA_DOKPLOY_TOKEN=local-dev-token` into a Dockerfile fails this test in CI. Distroless static-debian12:nonroot is the canonical runtime image for any CGO_ENABLED=0 Go binary in this repo (UID/GID 65532, no shell, no package manager); HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted because the image has no curl/wget/sh — orchestrators probe /healthz and /readyz over HTTP directly. The BuildKit cache mounts (`--mount=type=cache,target=/go/pkg/mod` and `/root/.cache/go-build`) require the `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7` directive at the top of the file; without that line the syntax silently falls back to legacy and the cache mounts become no-ops. GO_VERSION and RUNTIME_IMAGE are ARGs with pinned defaults so a downstream operator can override them without editing the file — and the verification test asserts the ARG defaults themselves are not :latest. The .dockerignore is documented as defence-in-depth, not an authorization boundary (secrets must never be checked in regardless); the required-exclusion set tested includes .git, .env, secrets/, *.pem, *.key. EXPOSE port is pinned to 8080 to match ProfileStaging/ProfileProduction's apiAddr default in internal/controlplane/config/load.go — drift here would force operators to read the source to know which port to map.
