ralph/codex-output/iteration-000017-20260518-210322.log:804:- Security-verification SSRF blocklist pattern, validate-port instance (BE-0349 pattern, internal/controlplane/validate/{ssrf.go, ssrf_static_test.go, ssrf_test.go, fuzz_test.go, AGENTS.md}; SIXTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST in the validate package — confirms the template scales to "this pure validator MUST funnel its parsed host through a named blocklist helper" invariants in addition to single-function-hardening (BE-0346), per-call-site predicate scans (BE-0344/0345/0347), and absence-of-API scans (BE-0348). Six SSRF-flavoured rules this instance surfaces that the earlier five do NOT capture: (a) The analyzer is a per-function call-presence scan rather than a per-call-site scan or absence-of-API scan: each of {URL, GitRepoURL} is parsed via findFuncDecl, its body walked with ast.Inspect, and the predicate "calls disallowedSSRFHost by exact identifier" must hold. Exact-identifier matching is the load-bearing rule — a synthetic "calls disallowedHost (look-alike)" fixture is in the self-check so a future refactor that renames the helper inside one validator (but not the other) fails the static guard; substring or prefix matching here would mask the regression. (b) The reason text is a closed set, NOT a templated string with the host interpolated. Nine canonical reasons (8 IP-range classifications + 1 hostname classification) live in package-private constants emitted by ipReason / disallowedSSRFHost. The runtime test pins the set via canonicalSSRFReasons + isCanonicalSSRFReason and asserts every rejection is set-membership equal — strictly stronger than substring redaction checks, because words like "localhost" / "metadata" / "loopback" / "private" appear in the classification text BY DESIGN and would false-positive a substring "host did not appear in reason" guard. The BE-0343 redaction-fixture-collision pattern (choose canary values that don't collide with stable reason phrases) is the predecessor; the closed-set assertion is the next-strongest form. (c) Reject IP literals via net.ParseIP first, then hostnames; the order matters because an IP literal can never be made safe by downstream DNS, so the unconditional-reject decision happens before any hostname rule applies. The IP classifier uses Go stdlib's IsUnspecified / IsLoopback / IsLinkLocalUnicast / IsLinkLocalMulticast / IsMulticast / IsPrivate as the first cut, then adds the four IPv4 ranges stdlib does NOT classify (CGNAT 100.64/10, benchmark 198.18/15, limited broadcast 255.255.255.255 — 169.254/16 is already covered by IsLinkLocalUnicast). Whitelisting IsPrivate alone would miss the cloud-metadata range (169.254.169.254 is link-local, not private). (d) Hostname forbidden set is asymmetric: an exact-match map (forbiddenSSRFHostnames) for {localhost, ip6-localhost, ip6-loopback, metadata, metadata.google.internal, metadata.aws.internal, 169.254.169.254} and a leading-dot suffix list (forbiddenSSRFHostSuffixes) for {.localhost, .internal, .local, .localdomain}. The leading dot on suffixes is required — a bare ".local" suffix without the dot would false-positive on "flocal.example.com". TestSSRFHostnameListsAreCanonical is a sealing-trap pinning required entries AND the leading-dot invariant; dropping any documented entry fails the build. (e) GitRepoURL has THREE entry shapes (https:// / ssh:// / scp-like git@host:path) and the SSRF check must fire on ALL THREE: the analyzer pins the presence of ONE disallowedSSRFHost call in the function body, but the runtime test exercises each shape independently (https loopback, ssh link-local v6, scp .internal, etc.) so a future split that drops the call from the scp branch alone — analyzer still happy because the https branch still has it — surfaces in the runtime table. The lesson: presence-of-call analyzers are necessary but not sufficient; per-shape runtime coverage is the second wall. (f) IPv6 literals in URL form MUST use the bracketed `[fd00::1]` shape because url.Parse with a colon-bearing v6 address outside brackets parses the colons as port separators; the runtime test fixtures call this out explicitly. The disallowedSSRFHost helper strips one set of surrounding brackets so it accepts BOTH the URL-extracted (already unbracketed) form AND a literal bracketed form a caller might supply directly. Layered defence is explicit in AGENTS.md: this is the PURE validator layer (no DNS) and a second wall in the HTTP/git client at fetch time is REQUIRED to catch DNS-rebinding (a public name resolving to an internal IP at fetch time). The validator is the first wall; the runtime resolved-IP re-check is a separate story.
