an a silently-skipped one. Document the layering explicitly in the file's package-doc block AND in the test file's table-header comment, so the next reader doesn't add an unreachable case and then debug for an hour. (c) The reserved-name lists must DELIBERATELY EXCLUDE the RFC 2606 documentation-only suffixes (`.test`, `.example`, `.invalid`) from the SUFFIX walk while KEEPING them in the EXACT-MATCH map. Reason: those suffixes are not publicly routable so they cannot intercept real customer traffic — they are a squatting threat (bounded by the `service_domains` UNIQUE (hostname, path) constraint) rather than a takeover threat. The httpapi service-domains test fixtures across ~7 files (`api.canonical.example`, `domain-matrix-recognisable-needle.example`, etc.) use `.example` per RFC convention; adding `.example` to the suffix walk would break every one of those tests, and the cost is asymmetric: a real takeover defence would require migrating the fixtures whereas a softer squatting defence is bounded by the UNIQUE constraint and adds no value to either threat model. Document the omission AND the asymmetry in the helper's package-doc, the AGENTS.md section, and the test file's blocked-hosts table comment — so a future reader doesn't "fix" the omission and re-break the fixtures. (d) The wildcard interaction: `Domain` strips the leading `*.` BEFORE my helper sees the host. With wildcards enabled, `*.appspot.com` reduces to `appspot.com` which the exact-match map rejects — a wildcard makes the takeover STRICTLY WORSE (the bare suffix locks one routing entry, the wildcard locks every sub-tenant of that suffix) so the wildcard rejection is load-bearing and gets its own test (`TestDomainRejectsTakeoverTargetsForWildcard`). The helper ALSO defensively strips a leading `*.` itself so it remains correct for any future caller that passes the raw value. (e) The redaction backstop (`TestDomainTakeoverReasonsAreValueFree`) must plant the canary as a SUB-LABEL of a reserved-SUFFIX